stname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'BAUMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3760c207-ccb2-59f9-b072-00ac22da233f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3760c207-ccb2-59f9-b072-00ac22da233f'}, {'abstract': '“Dreamlands: Immersive Cinema and Art, 1905-2016,” at the Whitney Museum of American Art, means to dismantle the cinematic givens we take for granted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/arts/design/diving-into-movie-palaces-of-the-mind-at-the-whitney.html', 'snippet': '“Dreamlands: Immersive Cinema and Art, 1905-2016,” at the Whitney Museum of American Art, means to dismantle the cinematic givens we take for granted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the movie camera emerged around the turn of the 20th century, it quickly became the miracle that never stopped giving. It attracted scientists, the news industry and entertainers. It generated its own forms of commerce, wealth and celebrity and, for a while, inspired its own architecture, the luxurious movie palace. It was itself the focus of constant innovation, from the advent of sound, color and 3-D, to digitalization, which let smartphones and other devices incorporate both filmless cameras and small screens — hand-held movie palaces. And from the very beginning, creative people of all types grasped the cinema as an artistic outlet that would transform traditional storytelling and popular culture while giving a new focus to the international avant-garde. That group soon set about taking liberties with all aspects of the miracle: the camera, film and projector and the ways they could be manipulated to alter experiences of time, light, space and self.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DREAMLAND/02DREAMLAND-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Diving Into Movie Palaces of the Mind at the Whitney', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Diving Into Movie Palaces of the Mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Museum of American Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iles, Chrissie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dreamlands: Immersive Cinema and Art, 1905-2016 (Exhib