nzied stampede at the door and fierce competition among collectors, the mood was decidedly more muted and buyers were taking their time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/arts/design/at-art-basel-miami-beach-fewer-dabblers-but-deliberative-sales.html', 'snippet': 'Instead of a frenzied stampede at the door and fierce competition among collectors, the mood was decidedly more muted and buyers were taking their time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI BEACH — The signs were subtle but unmistakable: dealers sitting down in their booths, checking their cellphones, even — could it be? — eating a salad.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1057, 'width': 1956, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02iNSIDERART/02iNSIDERART-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Art Basel Miami Beach, Fewer Dabblers but Deliberative Sales', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Subdued Art Basel Means Fewer Dabblers, but Deliberative Sales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art Basel Miami Beach', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-01T22:29:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7509d96e-6279-5586-a70f-4b1e3be634e8', 'word_count': 1033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7509d96e-6279-5586-a70f-4b1e3be634e8'}, {'abstract': 'A dark maturation unfolds in this story, from a child coolly absorbing an anatomy lesson to a traumatized adult taking on a more participatory role in death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/movies/the-eyes-of-my-mother-review.html', 'snippet': 'A dark maturation unfolds in this story, from a child coolly absorbing an anatomy lesson to a traumatized adult taking on a more participatory role in death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the very first shot, Nicolas Pesce’s “The Eyes of My Mother” unsettles as a broken woman staggers along a deserted country road before collapsing in front of an oncoming truck. It will be a long time before we learn who she is or what has happened to her; meantime, there are more than enough horrors to keep us occupied.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 251}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02EYES/02EYES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: A Woman’s Horrific Unraveling in ‘The Eyes of My Mother’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Eyes of My Mother (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pesce, Nicolas (1990- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-01T22:29:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/350f6e7d-f427-5b5b-9747-7cbcd6f4fedc', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/350f6e7d-f427-5b5b-9747-7cbcd6f4fedc'}, {'abstract': 'In this Aleksey Mizgirev film set in 1860 St. Petersburg, an ex-nobleman makes his living as a proxy in honor matches of shooting skill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/movies/the-duelist-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this Aleksey Mizgirev film set in 1860 St. Petersburg, an ex-nobleman makes his living as a proxy in honor matches of shooting skill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Russian-born author Vladimir Nabokov more than once complained about the conventions of depicting Russian duels in non-Russian works of art — noblemen with their backs turned to each other, walking 10 paces ahead, and so on. Not how it was done, the great writer insisted. “The Duelist,” a Russian-made picture about a former nobleman making his living as a proxy in honor-restoring matches of shooting skill, never shows such a duel. In this movie, directed by Aleksey Mizgirev and set in 1860 St. Petersburg, the fighters meet at close range, sometimes stand separated by a screen, and face off in other visually interesting ways. I still don’t think Nabokov would have approved — he had better taste in movies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02DUELIST/02DUELIST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', '