er': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8d87652-2400-5235-850e-1d4cb81f4fa1', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8d87652-2400-5235-850e-1d4cb81f4fa1'}, {'abstract': '“The Undoing Project: A Friendship That Changed Our Minds” looks at the groundbreaking collaboration between the psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/01/books/review-michael-lewis-on-two-well-matched-but-finally-mismatched-men.html', 'snippet': '“The Undoing Project: A Friendship That Changed Our Minds” looks at the groundbreaking collaboration between the psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among Daniel Kahneman’s many imaginative contributions to the field of psychology is something called the “peak-end rule,” which holds that our memories of any given experience are defined not by how we felt about it moment to moment, but how we felt as it ended and how it felt at its most intense. Say you go to Italy, and the first five days are blighted by rain, but the last two are ablaze with sunshine. You are likely to remember that trip far more fondly than had it been the other way around.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 831}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 740, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/02/arts/02BOOK/02BOOK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1459, 'width': 1054, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Michael Lewis on Two Well Matched (but Finally Mismatched) Men', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Men, Mismatched Yet Perfectly Paired', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Undoing Project: A Friendship That Changed Our Minds (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kahneman, Daniel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tversky, Amos', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-01T22:58:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Senior', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Senior', 'qualifier': N