e': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35ad93bb-362a-5077-9105-d1e8e818500f', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35ad93bb-362a-5077-9105-d1e8e818500f'}, {'abstract': 'Paulo Coelho’s “The Spy,” a fictional retelling of the life of Mata Hari, is No.18 on the extended hardcover fiction list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/02/books/review/the-story-behind-this-weeks-best-sellers.html', 'snippet': 'Paulo Coelho’s “The Spy,” a fictional retelling of the life of Mata Hari, is No.18 on the extended hardcover fiction list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Bed: We can affectionately tease Paulo Coelho, right? For one thing, the man seems impervious to it — his novel “The Alchemist,” after all, has sold more than 65 million copies even though it’s essentially a collection of motivational bromides. (“When we love, we always strive to become better than we are.” This meets the fortune cookie test: It would be improved by adding “in bed.”) For another thing, Coelho seems to be in on the joke. Here’s part of the biographical note from his latest novel, “The Spy,” new at No.\u202f18 on the extended hardcover fiction list: “He has flirted with death, escaped madness, dallied with drugs, withstood torture, experimented with magic and alchemy, studied philosophy and religion, read voraciously, lost and recovered his faith, and experienced the pain and pleasure of love. In searching for his own place in the world, he has discovered answers for the challenges that everyone faces.” If this isn’t meant at least 50 percent in jest — again, it helps to append “in bed” to each item — then Coelho’s pharmacological dalliance may not be entirely a thing of the past.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i