', 'url': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/03/us/04KENTUCKY-1/04KENTUCKY-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cubans in Louisville, Ky., Quietly Mark Castro’s Death', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Death of Castro, Cubans in Kentucky Aim to ‘Leave All That Behind’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cuban-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisville (Ky)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Fidel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-03T20:04:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheryl Gay Stolberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheryl', 'middlename': 'Gay', 'lastname': 'Stolberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbf5adf9-1da2-5a4f-9e56-997ceed3572f', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbf5adf9-1da2-5a4f-9e56-997ceed3572f'}, {'abstract': 'Socially responsible investing makes good business sense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/03/opinion/sunday/cashing-in-on-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'Socially responsible investing makes good business sense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’ve saved your money and amassed a surplus. You’ve read a few books on investing and gleaned the basics — the importance of diversification, of investing for the long term, and of buying and holding rather than trying to beat the market. But you also know that human-caused climate change will (if it hasn’t already) start eroding economic output. Extreme weather, droughts and crop failures could mean mass migration and political instability. As Henry Paulson, the former Treasury secretary, recently put it, the “greenhouse-gas crisis” won’t burst like the housing bubble of 2008 because “climate change is more subtle and cruel.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/opinion/sunday/04velasquez/04velasquez-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/04/opinion/sunday/04velasquez/04velasquez-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'