 'value': 'Carbon Disclosure Project (CDP)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-03T20:17:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Moises Velasquez-Manoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Moises', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Velasquez-Manoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f60b0d8-8f53-59f6-bb91-7b8fc19e482d', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f60b0d8-8f53-59f6-bb91-7b8fc19e482d'}, {'abstract': '20-something Republicans have clustered in reading groups sponsored by conservative foundations. Liberals should imitate them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/03/opinion/sunday/can-i-go-to-great-books-camp.html', 'snippet': '20-something Republicans have clustered in reading groups sponsored by conservative foundations. Liberals should imitate them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KATE Havard is taking a break from American conservatism. “I’m not sure what’s happening with my party right now,” she said from Jerusalem, where she is studying Hebrew. “I need a timeout.” When she returns to her job in Washington at the Foundation for the Defense of Democracies, she’ll seek refuge among a small group of young conservatives who believe that studying the history of ideas helps them keep Donald J. Trump in perspective.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/opinion/sunday/04worthensub/04worthensub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard',