ract': 'This week’s episode felt like a French film set among the haute bourgeoisie mixed with a chilly thriller.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/04/arts/television/the-affair-season-2-episode-3-recap.html', 'snippet': 'This week’s episode felt like a French film set among the haute bourgeoisie mixed with a chilly thriller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Through two seasons, “The Affair” charted a course between melodrama and comedy of manners, with a ticky-tacky mystery thrown in to keep the plot percolating. Season 3, so far, has been different — a little less ripe, a little more serious. The season’s third episode began with a chapter that felt like a French film set among the haute bourgeoisie — appropriate for the point of view of Juliette, the visiting professor of medieval literature — and ended with a chapter that played like a chilly psychological thriller, with Noah scanning the streets for the prison guard who he thinks is stalking him. Is this quieter approach an improvement? Personally, I could have used more Furkat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/watching/04affair/04affair-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/04/watching/04affair/04affair-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/watching/04affair/04affair-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/04/watching/04affair/04affair-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/