 {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer and Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94c1e122-ec34-5a1f-a63b-4fdcfbec93da', 'word_count': 1118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94c1e122-ec34-5a1f-a63b-4fdcfbec93da'}, {'abstract': 'A seething “Elektra.” Thomas Adès’s “The Exterminating Angel.” And who can forget those 100 bleating sheep at the Park Avenue Armory?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/07/arts/music/best-classical.html', 'snippet': 'A seething “Elektra.” Thomas Adès’s “The Exterminating Angel.” And who can forget those 100 bleating sheep at the Park Avenue Armory?', 'lead_paragraph': '‘ELEKTRA’ The fearless French director Patrice Chéreau died at 68 in 2013, a few months after his searing production of Strauss’s “Elektra” had its premiere at the Aix-en-Provence Festival in France. The Metropolitan Opera, one of the co-producers of this seething and psychologically piercing staging, presented it in April with a matchless cast headed by the gleaming soprano Nina Stemme in the title role. Esa-Pekka Salonen conducted the score with a composer’s insight into its complexities and splendors. It was an immediate Met milestone. [Read the review]', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/arts/11CLASSICALYEARSBEST-GRID/11CLASSICALYEARSBEST-GRID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/arts/11CLASSICALYEARSBEST-GRID/11CLASSICALYEARSBEST-GRID-thumbSta