Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/42d1eb9c-1339-5a67-9dac-6069102a8d95', 'word_count': 767, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42d1eb9c-1339-5a67-9dac-6069102a8d95'}, {'abstract': 'This loose, loopy and enjoyable seminar on the making of “The Wizard of Oz” combines live video and performance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/theater/elements-of-oz-review.html', 'snippet': 'This loose, loopy and enjoyable seminar on the making of “The Wizard of Oz” combines live video and performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A loose, loopy and enjoyable seminar on the making of “The Wizard of Oz” and its influence on pop culture, this Builders Association production, running through Dec. 18, combines live video and performance. Several famous scenes from the movie are more or less filmed anew before us; the digital video is dazzlingly pristine, and it’s fun to watch the wry comic impersonations of the original performances.. [Read the original review] 800-838-3006, 3ldnyc.org', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 783}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 785, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09THEATERLIST/09THEATERLIST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1570, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Beat a New Path Down a Familiar Yellow Brick Road in ‘Elements of Oz’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater: Beat a New Path Down a Familiar Yellow Brick Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Elements of Oz (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Builders Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '3LD Art & Technology Center', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-08T22:13:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d6e5aa4-254d-5330-a2d3-0cbe8abd4e88', 'word_count': 72, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d6e5aa4-254d-5330-a2d3-0cbe8abd4e88'}, {'abstract': 'The German artist Hanne Darboven’s magnum opus offers a personal, imperfect vision that absorbs, but never fully clarifies, the 20th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/arts/design/hanna-darboven-kulturgeschichte.html', 'snippet': 'The German artist Hanne Darboven’s magnum opus offers a personal, imperfect vision that absorbs, but never fully clarifies, the 20th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What force drives societal disaster: too little order, or too much of it? These days of presidential interregnum have seen newspapers’ opinion pages fill with warnings of tyranny and autocracy, fueled by the election of a man whose campaign rhetoric showed disregard for normal rules of conduct. Yet, for many philosophers of the postwar era, too much order seemed just as dangerous as too little. You may dream of perfectly rational government, but fitting the complexities of life into dispassionate systems poses its own hazards. Taken to extremes, as Theodor W. Adorno insisted, the Enlightenment principles of reason and logic would pave the road to Auschwitz.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 567, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09DARBOVEN/09DARBOVEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1134, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Perils of Order, Taken to the Extreme', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Perils of Order, Taken to the Extreme', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farago, Jason', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darboven, Hanne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dia Art Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-08T22:18:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Farago', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farago', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc1d32ad-8327-5643-867b-0585496369e3', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc1d32ad-8327-5643-867b-0585496369e3'}, {'abstract': 'Jaco Van Dormael’s satire follows a bored deity who, from his apartment in Brussels, plays jokes on humans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/movies/the-brand-new-testament-review.html', 'snippet': 'Jaco Van Dormael’s satire follows a bored deity who, from his apartment in Brussels, plays jokes on humans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Belgian filmmaker Jaco Van Dormael’s wickedly amusing religious satire, “The Brand New Testament,” God (Benoît Poelvoorde) is a snarling, meanspirited bully who rules the universe from an apartment in Brussels. Inside his locked office, surrounded by walls of card files, the tyrannical, perpetually bored deity sits behind a computer and plays nasty practical jokes on humans. A favorite pastime is contriving Laws of Annoyance, like making sure that when a piece of toast falls, it always lands with the jelly side down.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09BRANDNEW1/09BRANDNEW1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1420, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: God Is Alive and Crabby, According to ‘The Brand New Testament’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'God Is in Brussels,  Alive and Crabby', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Brand New Testament (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Dormael, Jaco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-08T22:20:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdd71bb2-b45b-5d56-b995-bfe87616f57e', 'word_count': 534, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdd71bb2-b45b-5d56-b995-bfe87616f57e'}, {'abstract': 'A teenage girl and her downtrodden mother struggle to build a normal life, but any progress they make seems to unravel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/movies/all-we-had-review-katie-holmes.html', 'snippet': 'A teenage girl and her downtrodden mother struggle to build a normal life, but any progress they make seems to unravel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Homelessness, alcoholism, real estate chicanery, the financial crisis, teenage drug abuse and transgender discrimination: These are some of the themes dutifully trotted out and glossed over in the screen adaptation of Annie Weatherwax’s 2014 novel, “All We Had.” Although sensitively handled in the film, it all adds up to an overload of baggage for a well-meaning but formulaic movie with not enough time to transcend predictable clichés.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09ALLIHAD/09ALLIHAD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Katie Holmes Directs Her First Feature, ‘All We Had’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tenuous World, but in It Together', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'All We Had (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Katie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Luke', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weatherwax, Annie', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-08T22:27:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/27227d65-7888-54be-9a53-d740e34d1c0f', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27227d65-7888-54be-9a53-d740e34d1c0f'}, {'abstract': 'This is MoMA’s first show that traces the progress of one art movement, assembled entirely from the museum’s collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/arts/design/the-russian-avant-garde-rising-in-a-revolutionary-impulse.html', 'snippet': 'This is MoMA’s first show that traces the progress of one art movement, assembled entirely from the museum’s collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Museum of Modern Art more or less set the standard for the broad swath of media typically collected by a fine-art museum by creating the first departments of photography, film, architecture and design. But then a weird primness set in: Departmental holdings were rarely allowed to mix in the galleries. The divisions remained intact even as the hybrid forms of 1970s art sowed confusion. (Performance art, video, Conceptual Art, anyone?) Then, to the dismay of many, the system was once more reinforced by the museum’s 2004 redesign, which gave all departments their own dedicated galleries.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09JPMOMA6/09JPMOMA6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/09/arts/09JPMOMA6/09JPMOMA6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'cred