news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a25ead83-45aa-5ed3-aee6-d84e4af80069', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a25ead83-45aa-5ed3-aee6-d84e4af80069'}, {'abstract': 'A reader asks about a park-within-a-park near the Korean War Memorial in Battery Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/nyregion/what-happened-to-walloon-park.html', 'snippet': 'A reader asks about a park-within-a-park near the Korean War Memorial in Battery Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Before Hurricane Sandy, a lovely, messy little circular park-within-a-park near the Korean War Memorial in Battery Park had a sign that labeled it Walloon Park. That area reopened just last year and is now labeled Jerusalem Cove. It features a vegetable and flower garden as well as the cedars and the circular labyrinth that were there before (though the cedars are half-dead, poor things, from hurricane damage). What happened to the name Walloon Park?', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11FYI/11FYI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11FYI/11FYI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11FYI/11FYI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'c