00:46:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/630a4ff2-30da-55a2-8ab9-5cc503630c5c', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/630a4ff2-30da-55a2-8ab9-5cc503630c5c'}, {'abstract': 'Is it boredom, anxiety or an attempt to fill a schnauzerpoo-size void that drives us to buy things we will never use — especially things at holiday markets?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/nyregion/grasping-for-sanity-in-the-season-of-fiscal-impairment.html', 'snippet': 'Is it boredom, anxiety or an attempt to fill a schnauzerpoo-size void that drives us to buy things we will never use — especially things at holiday markets?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I just bought an item called “My Little Steamer.” Obviously I was fiscally impaired. Would I buy a product called “My Little Sneakers” or “My Little Automobile”? This steamer is about as effective as taking a sip of hot tea and then exhaling over a garment. Actually, exhaling would be more effective because then you might spot the garment and be forced to take it to a dry cleaner.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11MISINFORMED1/11MISINFORMED1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1450, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grasping for Sanity in the Season of Fiscal Impairment', 'kicker': 'I Was Misinformed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Season for Fiscal Impairment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wadler, Joyce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop-Up Stores and Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-09T00:48:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Wadler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wadler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2718332c-4d0e-5c9d-ae85-f814244ab594', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2718332c-4d0e-5c9d-ae85-f814244ab594'}, {'abstract': 'The 31-year-old baritone, who is also a professor at Brooklyn College Conservatory of Music, immerses himself in scores and then unwinds with comfort food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/08/nyregion/malcolm-merriweather-dessoff-choirs.html', 'snippet': 'The 31-year-old baritone, who is also a professor at Brooklyn College Conservatory of Music, immerses himself in scores and then unwinds with comfort food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For members of the Handel-industrial complex, it’s showtime. From now until you-know-when, choirs singing the “Messiah” will give canned holiday music a run for its money. Malcolm J. Merriweather, 31, who made his professional solo debut at 10 singing the “Messiah” in Buffalo (he took home $10), will be paying it forward on Dec. 17, when he conducts the nearly century-old Dessoff Choirs in a sing-in with audience participation at the Union Theological Seminary in Manhattan. Mr. Merriweather, who begins most Sundays in his 15th-floor apartment in Harlem, is an accomplished baritone as well as the choirs’ musical director. He is also an assistant professor and director of choirs at Brooklyn College Conservatory of Music of the City University of New York.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/nyregion/11ROUTINE1/11ROUTINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1444, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Malcolm Merriweather, Musical Director of the Dessoff Choirs, Spends His Sundays', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Immersed in Scores, Concerts and Classes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dessoff Choirs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merriweather, Malcolm J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handel, George Frederick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-09T00:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, '