nization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12259717-409d-5a35-a4bf-17430271f315', 'word_count': 1077, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12259717-409d-5a35-a4bf-17430271f315'}, {'abstract': 'The Chuck Jones method for dealing with a tweeter in chief.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/09/opinion/twitter-fingers-headed-for-the-white-house.html', 'snippet': 'The Chuck Jones method for dealing with a tweeter in chief.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About 19 minutes after Chuck Jones, head of the steelworkers union at the Carrier plant in Indianapolis, went on CNN to correct Donald Trump’s inflated count of the jobs he’d saved there, the president-elect unleashed the hounds on Twitter. Anthony Scaramucci, a Trump transition official appearing on the same program, had barely finished saying, “What I admire about Mr. Jones, he’s fighting for the American worker,” when Mr. Trump tweeted, “Chuck Jones, who is President of United Steelworkers 1999, has done a terrible job representing workers.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/opinion/09fri3web/09fri3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/09/opinion/09fri3web/09fri3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/opinion/09fri3web/09fri3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/opinion/09fri3web/09fri3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLa