of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0c6b5b4-bd63-5f65-a0ad-b8620f282a2e', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0c6b5b4-bd63-5f65-a0ad-b8620f282a2e'}, {'abstract': 'Create an S-curve out of the blade. Don’t bow on the teeth side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/09/magazine/how-to-play-the-saw.html', 'snippet': 'Create an S-curve out of the blade. Don’t bow on the teeth side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just about any version of the familiar cutting tool will do — “any nonelectric, full-bladed saw,” says Natalia (the Saw Lady) Paruz, who has performed with dozens of orchestras internationally. “The longer the blade, the more notes it will have.” Paruz has three different saws specially made for musical performances, but she recommends starting with a standard carpenter’s saw of 26 inches. “It’s the easiest length, and you have about an octave and a half to work with,” Paruz says. “If you want more complex music, you need 32 inches. But if the saw is too long, it’s unwieldy, and you can’t really handle it.” Thinner blades are easier to manipulate. You need to be able to bend the thing easily.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11tip/11mag-11tip-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1052, 'width': 1497, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Play the Saw', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Play the Saw', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowe, Jaime', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Musical Instruments', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paruz, Natalia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-09T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jaime Lowe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jaime', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5fa3905-8d37-513b-be3e-85849d3d1bc8', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5fa3905-8d37-513b-be3e-85849d3d1bc8'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Dickens appreciated liquor in both his prose and his life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/09/magazine/a-victorian-toast.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Dickens appreciated liquor in both his prose and his life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He’s no seasonal superstar like Scrooge, but let’s spare a thought for Krook, the unfortunate rag-and-bottle man in “Bleak House,” Charles Dickens’s great novel of the law and its tortuous machinations. A drinker of gin in colossal quantity, Krook perishes after going up in flames, the victim of the most infamous instance of spontaneous human combustion in English literature. Some draw a connection between his immoderate intake of alcohol and his predisposition for auto-immolation; I keep in mind that the cheap, corrosive rotgut of Krook’s place and time was different from the nice stuff we take with tonic today.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11drink/11drink-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11drink/11drink-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11drink/11drink-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11drink/11drink-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11drink/11drink-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 754}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/magazine/11drink/11drink-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty