oir is No. 3 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says she has carefully calibrated just how revealing she wants to be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I Will Follow: Let’s get the beta male confession out of the way — I used to watch “Gilmore Girls” with my wife, and I liked it. The characters’ rapport and repartee (rapportee?) were like throwbacks to the great “Thin Man” movies, and the series cultivated such a literary vibe during its original seven-year run that websites compiled vast reading lists from its allusions. All of which is to say: When I told my wife that Lauren Graham, who played Lorelai Gilmore, would be appearing at the 92nd Street Y in New York to promote her memoir, “Talking as Fast as I Can” (new at No.\u202f3 on the hardcover nonfiction list), we were there in a trice. Picture William Shatner speaking to a crowd of “Star Trek” fans, only 98 percent female, and you’ll get the idea: lots of fellowship and laughter and screaming, with the center of attention gamely baffled and entertained by it all. “I love you, Lauren!” one woman shouted, and Graham, grinning widely at the sea of cellphone flash bulbs, deadpanned, “I love your adherence to ‘The use of photography is strictly prohibited.’\u200a”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/books/review/18graham/18graham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/18/books/review/18graham/18graham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/books/review/18graham/18graham-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': Non