: 1}, {'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3666651-2e6f-507c-b903-b895bdadc6ea', 'word_count': 1207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3666651-2e6f-507c-b903-b895bdadc6ea'}, {'abstract': 'A co-op has problems with a grocery store that attracts pigeons and rats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/10/realestate/how-can-a-co-op-shoo-away-a-pigeon-complaint.html', 'snippet': 'A co-op has problems with a grocery store that attracts pigeons and rats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Our co-op on the Upper West Side  is next door to a grocery store. Our back courtyard shares open space with  the grocery store’s air vents and cooling system. Pigeons are  nesting around the store’s cooling system, providing food for rats. Our co-op hired an exterminator, but so long as the nests remain, the problem continues. The store’s management company has not returned our calls. When we called 311, we received a warning from the city that our building was in violation of health codes and would face fines if the pigeon problem was not resolved. We called 311 again to explain. The operator opened a new service complaint, but we have heard nothing back. That was six months ago. What recourse do we have?', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/realestate/11-ASK-1/11-ASK-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/11/realestate/11-ASK-1/11-ASK-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/11/realestate/11-ASK-1/11-ASK-1-thu