inal': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/079bc45d-21fe-5bec-a59d-df5d6b2160ee', 'word_count': 1607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/079bc45d-21fe-5bec-a59d-df5d6b2160ee'}, {'abstract': 'For the religious right, vouchers are a way to dismantle public education.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/13/opinion/betsy-devos-and-gods-plan-for-schools.html', 'snippet': 'For the religious right, vouchers are a way to dismantle public education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — At the rightmost edge of the Christian conservative movement, there are those who dream of turning the United States into a Christian republic subject to “biblical laws.” In the unlikely figure of Donald J. Trump, they hope to have found their greatest champion yet. He wasn’t “our preferred candidate,” the Christian nationalist David Barton said in June, but he could be “God’s candidate.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/13/opinion/13stewart/13stewart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/13/opinion/13stewart/13stewart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/13/opinion/13stewart/13stewart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/13/opinion/13stewart/13stewart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, '