', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tejal Rao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tejal', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6918658c-ca3e-5973-b250-348ada58a511', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6918658c-ca3e-5973-b250-348ada58a511'}, {'abstract': 'Why scientists and startups are tinkering with our most popular legume.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/15/magazine/is-it-possible-to-make-a-less-allergenic-peanut.html', 'snippet': 'Why scientists and startups are tinkering with our most popular legume.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 1987, Wesley Burks, a pediatric allergist in Little Rock, decided to enroll in a graduate-level biochemistry course at the University of Arkansas. Twice a week, instead of driving to his laboratory at the Arkansas Children’s Hospital, he headed to the medical-school campus about a mile away. There, he joined some 20 students learning to pipette liquid extracts into gels and stain them with pigments to figure out which protein molecules they contained. Burks had only recently completed his medical fellowship, where he studied why some children have allergic reactions to milk and eggs. Now he was throwing himself into biochemistry so he could be the first to figure out what it was about peanuts that made people so allergic. “It’s easy to say you want to do something that’ll change kids’ lives,” Burks says. “But you have to focus on an area to really get it done.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 484, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 484}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 826, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18peanut1/18mag-18peanut-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1651, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It Possible to Make a Less Allergenic Peanut?', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Peanut’s Progress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Peanuts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khamsi, Roxanne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALLERGIES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biotechnology and Bioengineering', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burks, A Wesley', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-15T10