l', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'JONES', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/86fe6d9b-7428-5011-8912-b60497f68759', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/86fe6d9b-7428-5011-8912-b60497f68759'}, {'abstract': 'The Times critic Michael Kimmelman reflects on how architects have changed New York City this year, making it more livable, humane and energizing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/15/arts/comfort-and-joy-in-bricks-and-mortar.html', 'snippet': 'The Times critic Michael Kimmelman reflects on how architects have changed New York City this year, making it more livable, humane and energizing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the ribbon-cutting at the Washington Heights Library this month, the son of a former custodian returned to his old home. He grew up in what used to be the caretaker’s apartment on the third floor. While his father shoveled coal at night to heat the place, he would go downstairs to read about boat building and navigating by the stars. Ronald Clark went on to become the first in his family to graduate from high school and college, and decades later he built himself a boat and navigated by the stars that he had learned about in the library under him.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/16/arts/16JPBUILDINGS4/16JPBUILDINGS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/16/arts/16JPBUILDINGS4/16JPBUILDINGS4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/16/arts/16JPBUILDINGS4/16JPBUILDINGS4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height'