 {'abstract': 'The director discusses a scene from the science fiction film featuring Amy Adams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/15/movies/denis-villeneuve-narrates-a-scene-from-arrival.html', 'snippet': 'The director discusses a scene from the science fiction film featuring Amy Adams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this scene from “Arrival,” a linguist (Amy Adams) and a team of experts try to communicate with aliens who have planted their ships in several spots around the world. In an interview, the director Denis Villeneuve discussed why he and his production designer decided against using green screen, and how puppeteers were used to mimic the movement of ailens. Here are excerpts from that conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Denis Villeneuve Narrates a Scene From ‘Arrival’', 'kicker': 'Anatomy of a Scene', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Villeneuve, Denis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, Amy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Arrival (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-15T19:04:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4385f1b0-0dcf-53ce-b2fd-55fb6de172f0', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4385f1b0-0dcf-53ce-b2fd-55fb6de172f0'}, {'abstract': 'The Anthology Film Archives is giving viewers a chance to discover this long-forgotten director with a beautiful restoration of her 1916 film “The Dumb Girl of Portici.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/15/movies/lois-weber-eloquent-filmmaker-of-the-silent-screen.html', 'snippet': 'The Anthology Film Archives is giving viewers a chance to discover this long-forgotten director with a beautiful restoration of her 1916 film “The Dumb Girl of Portici.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once upon a Hollywood time, one of filmdom’s biggest directors was Lois Weber. Woodrow Wilson was president, and women couldn’t have voted for him even if they had wanted to, but inside the movie industry, women thrived, and Weber thrived above all others. An auteur before that word entered the cinematic lexicon, she wrote, directed and edited films and was admired for her sensitive work with actors, her on-set meticulousness and her stories about women. Her name was invoked alongside the likes of D. W. Griffith, yet, like most female directors of that era, she faded into obscurity.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/15/arts/17LOIS-WEBER/17LOIS-WEBER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/15/arts/17LOIS-WEBER/17LOIS-WEBER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/15/arts/17LOIS-WEBER/17LOIS-WEBER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/15/arts/17LOIS-WEBER/17LOIS-WEBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/15/arts/17LOIS-WEBER/17LOIS-WEBER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 475}}, {'ran