ne, 'lastname': 'Hubbard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hicks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a52e6636-6307-5049-a1e9-61f38d602e88', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a52e6636-6307-5049-a1e9-61f38d602e88'}, {'abstract': 'By advocating around a problem that Mayor Bill de Blasio has seemed so ineffective in mitigating, Ms. Quinn has put herself in a position to mount a new challenge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/16/nyregion/christine-quinn-win.html', 'snippet': 'By advocating around a problem that Mayor Bill de Blasio has seemed so ineffective in mitigating, Ms. Quinn has put herself in a position to mount a new challenge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sept. 11, 2013, Christine C. Quinn, who had just suffered a rough and at one time entirely unimaginable defeat in New York City’s Democratic primary race for mayor, headed downtown to the National September 11 Memorial and turned off her phone. A few hours later, she realized she had missed several calls from Hillary Clinton’s office. Mrs. Clinton had phoned to extend her sympathies to a female candidate who had received only 16 percent of the vote among women, had been criticized for an abrasive demeanor, a grating voice, a poorly managed campaign and a failure to telegraph a resonant message that could speak to the fear and disgust surrounding the rise in economic inequality. Only in the rear view, of course, do we see this as preamble.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/nyregion/18BIG1/18BIG1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1397, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Homeless Issue, Christine Quinn Gets Back in the Conversation', 'kicker': 'Big City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quinn Finds  Her Next Act', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Christine C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Win NYC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-16T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/222ef5fc-c23a-5b42-8e81-35d9c35e6dca', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/222ef5fc-c23a-5b42-8e81-35d9c35e6dca'}, {'abstract': 'Building a new home on the land where family members were killed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/16/magazine/a-grim-discovery-in-el-mozote.html', 'snippet': 'Building a new home on the land where family members were killed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many assumed I died in the massacre of December 1981, when government soldiers killed hundreds of people in El Mozote, my village in El Salvador. The victims were accused of collaborating with left-wing guerrillas, but most of them were women, children and old people. Soldiers tortured my family, poured gasoline on them and set my childhood home on fire. But I survived, because I was in San Salvador, working and studying for my accounting degree. It was only years later that I had thoughts of moving back.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 843}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 729, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18lives/18mag-18lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1067, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Grim Discovery in El Mozote', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Return to El Mozote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Salvador', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War and Revolution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Mozote (El Salvador)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-16T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'As told to Sarah Esther Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d51a136-1c86-58b4-bc0f-ce6cbac2f86e', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d51a136-1c86-58b4-bc0f-ce6cbac2f86e'}, {'abstract': 'How can we trust him in our homes when he won’t even release his birth certificate?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/16/magazine/judge-john-hodgman-on-santa-claus-conspiracies.html', 'snippet': 'How can we trust him in our homes when he won’t even release his birth certificate?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Spencer writes: I believe that Santa Claus is an elf. In “The Night Before Christmas,” Santa is described as “a right jolly old elf.” He is also said to travel by “miniature sleigh” led by “eight tiny reindeer,” suggesting he is elf-size. My co-worker Elena believes, as many others do, that Santa is a kind of superpowered human. Who is right?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18hodgman/18mag-18hodgman-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18hodgman/18mag-18hodgman-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18hodgman/18mag-18hodgman-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18hodgman/18mag-18hodgman-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 596, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18hodgman/18mag-18hodgman-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 596}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/18/magazine/18hodgman/18mag-18hodgman-t_CA0-jumbo.j