': 'New York Times music critics discuss how pop’s dominant delivery system changed the way music was made and consumed this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week on the Popcast, the New York Times pop team started to analyze what critic Jon Caramanica calls “a disruptive year in the pop music ecosystem” via the best albums of the year — powerful releases by some of the country’s most prodigious talents, including Beyoncé, Frank Ocean and Bon Iver. In each of those cases, the album was an evolutionary step for the artist in question, and also for pop music more broadly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/10/24/arts/17DRAKE/24DRAKE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Streaming, Exclusives and the Chainsmokers Made 2016 a Disruptive Year for Pop', 'kicker': 'Popcast', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-16T20:05:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bd3034c-bbfd-55e9-92ea-a827d249bb89', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bd3034c-bbfd-55e9-92ea-a827d249bb89'}, {'abstract': 'But for Mr. Evans, whose newest solo release is “Lifeblood,” the point of his music goes well beyond his breathtaking technique and brazen inventions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/16/arts/music/peter-evans-the-new-vanguard.html', 'snippet': 'But for Mr. Evans, whose newest solo release is “Lifeblood,” the point of his music goes well beyond his breathtaking technique and brazen inventions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peter Evans brought a piccolo trumpet to his lips at the start of a recent solo performance at Pioneer Works in Brooklyn, and within moments he’d cast a deep and disorienting spell. Standing on the concrete floor in semidarkness, he played one long, unbroken improvisation that defied the conventional limitations of the instrument, if not the laws of physics.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/17/arts/17EVANSVANGUARD/17EVANSVANGUARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name