name': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbcbf554-ee25-5910-b631-6c81b07134d6', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbcbf554-ee25-5910-b631-6c81b07134d6'}, {'abstract': 'Forwarding a jeering meme to people who agree with you is not political action.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/19/opinion/how-to-live-without-irony-for-real-this-time-.html', 'snippet': 'Forwarding a jeering meme to people who agree with you is not political action.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four years ago, shortly after the re-election of Barack Obama, I published an essay in The Stone called “How to Live Without Irony.” In it, I argued that the surplus of apolitical irony in a certain segment of American culture — most clearly expressed in the rise of hipsterism but visible in many other forms — had a dangerous potential. It represented a surrender to commercial and political forces that could lead to an emptiness of both the individual and collective psyche. “Historically, vacuums eventually have been filled by something,” I wrote, “more often than not, a hazardous something.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/opinion/19stoneWeb/19stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/19/opinion/19stoneWeb/19stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/opinion/19stoneWeb/19stoneWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credi