', '_id': 'nyt://article/84aad9f9-824f-5e06-87f9-7ff787a6bcab', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84aad9f9-824f-5e06-87f9-7ff787a6bcab'}, {'abstract': 'Why the conflict over Pope Francis’s innovations has only just begun.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2016/12/19/amoris-laetitia-and-everything-after/', 'snippet': 'Why the conflict over Pope Francis’s innovations has only just begun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time for another intervention on matters Catholic, I’m afraid. Last week Austen Ivereigh, author of a very fine Pope Francis biography and frequent defender of the pontiff\xa0against conservative critics, wrote a piece for Crux announcing that the debate over “Amoris Laetitia” and communion for the remarried is essentially over, that intransigent conservatives are now as much “dissenters” from the papal magisterium as any liberal agitating for the ordination of women under John Paul II —\xa0and that long after the cardinals questioning Francis are no more “than a footnote in the history of this papacy, long after Ross Douthat’s predicted\xa0schism\xa0from the columns of the\xa0New York Times\xa0has failed to materialize, the next generation of priests\xa0will be applying\xa0the magnificent teaching of ‘Amoris Laetitia,’ and the noisy, angry strains of dissent will have faded\xa0into a distant memory.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amoris Laetitia and Everything After', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euthanasia and Assisted Suicide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benedict XVI', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'John Paul II', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North America', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-19T15:42:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93d95c54-abc2-59e0-bc69-d94c909010e7', 'word_count': 1461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93d95c54-abc2-59e0-bc69-d94c909010e7'}, {'abstract': 'In this season of giving, we asked some notably avid readers — who also happen to be poets, musicians, diplomats, filmmakers, novelists, actors and artists — to share the books that accompanied them through 2016.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2016/12/19/books/review/25year-in-reading.html', 'snippet': 'In this season of giving, we asked some notably avid readers — who also happen to be poets, musicians, diplomats, filmmakers, novelists, actors and artists — to share the books that accompanied them through 2016.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this season of giving, we asked some notably avid readers — who also happen to be poets, musicians, diplomats, filmmakers, novelists, actors and artists — to share the books that accompanied them through 2016.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/09/books/review/readersbooks_-slide-C7WG/readersbooks_-slide-C7WG-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75,