ation, claiming they had harassed the mother of a white nationalist leader.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/20/us/whitefish-montana-jews-daily-stormer.html', 'snippet': 'A white supremacist site listed the names of local Jews and their contact information, claiming they had harassed the mother of a white nationalist leader.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The police in Whitefish, Mont., said Tuesday that they have stepped up patrols and are working with the F.B.I. after a neo-Nazi and white supremacist website listed the names and contacts of local Jews, calling on readers to “take action” against them.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/us/20xp-whitefish/20xp-whitefish-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Neo-Nazi Posting, Police in Whitefish, Mont., Step Up Patrols', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Police in Montana Step Up Patrols After Neo-Nazi Posting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Whitefish (Mont)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Neo Nazi Groups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Daily Stormer', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spencer, Richard B (1978- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anglin, Andrew', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-20T17:45:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23c180c2-3dac-5cf2-9773-61ee4f05ee52', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23c180c2-3dac-5cf2-9773-61ee4f05ee52'}, {'abstract': 'The children’s books authors R.J. Palacio and Meg Medina talk to Maria Russo, The Times’ children’s books editor, about being — and choosing not to be — a Latina author. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/20/books/review/rj-palacio-and-meg-medina-talk-diversity-and-childrens-books.html', 'snippet': 'The children’s books authors R.J. Palacio and Meg Medina talk to Maria Russo, The Times’ children’s books editor, about being — and choosing not to be — a Latina author. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'R.J. Palacio and Meg Medina both grew up in Spanish-speaking households. As elementary-school students at P.S. 22 in Flushing, Queens, they were best friends. Medina moved away at 11, and they lost touch. Years later they discovered they had both grown up to become children’s book authors. Both have achieved renown: Palacio (whose real name is Raquel Jaramillo) wrote “Wonder,” the best-selling middle-grade novel many credit with launching a worldwide movement to teach kids to choose kindness. Medina has written several award-winning books for all age groups, most recently the young adult novel “Burn Baby Burn,” longlisted for the 2016 National Book Award and shortlisted for the Kirkus Prize. When they reconnected, Palacio and Medina found they still had much in common. But in one way they have taken different paths: Medina identifies as a Latina writer, writes about Latino characters, and speaks often about diversity issues. Palacio does not.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/books/review/19palacio/19palacio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/19/books/review/19palacio/19palacio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/books/review/19palacio/19palacio-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/19/books/review/19palacio/19palacio-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 6