pe': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Mather', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mather', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7993fd4b-5939-5808-8511-1974b2e5b47b', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7993fd4b-5939-5808-8511-1974b2e5b47b'}, {'abstract': 'To make a good puzzle, you need a good idea. Jeff Chen and Seth Geltman show us an example.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/20/crosswords/shoot-for-the-moon-ariadne-labyrinth-house-foolish.html', 'snippet': 'To make a good puzzle, you need a good idea. Jeff Chen and Seth Geltman show us an example.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEDNESDAY PUZZLE — As part of my college major in Strategic Guessing, also known to some as marketing, I took an advertising class where an ad’s Big Idea was as elusive as the Holy Grail. The professor’s methods were probably not the best way to go about encouraging creativity, but I can remember him systematically shooting down any idea for a class campaign that he did not believe was a Big Idea. That, in a nutshell, is probably why the vast majority of my graduating class became accountants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/21/crosswords/21wordplay-moldau/21wordplay-moldau-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/21/crosswords/21wordplay-moldau/21wordplay-moldau-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/21/crosswords/21wordplay-moldau/21wordplay-moldau-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thu