tent_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-21T16:27:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Watching', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yaa Gyasi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yaa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gyasi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3347622e-2f78-5c16-b05a-1f2c776d46a2', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3347622e-2f78-5c16-b05a-1f2c776d46a2'}, {'abstract': 'The Queens collective the Juice Crew is getting back together for reunion shows in Manhattan and Montclair, N.J.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/21/arts/music/the-juice-crew-a-rap-history-reunion.html', 'snippet': 'The Queens collective the Juice Crew is getting back together for reunion shows in Manhattan and Montclair, N.J.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By New York standards, the New Year’s Eve options are thin, so get your fix earlier in the week with a rap-history field trip: a reunion of the Juice Crew, the Queens collective responsible for so much essential music in the mid-to-late 1980s. Anchored by the producer Marley Marl, it included a wide range of provocative rappers. Big Daddy Kane was mercilessly smooth, Kool G Rap a rough-edged storyteller. Craig G was wildly improvisational, and MC Shan and Masta Ace were hard boasters. In Roxanne Shanté, they had one of rap’s foundational tough women, and in Biz Markie, one of rap’s foundational comedians. Individually, their careers took wildly different directions, but their collective legacy remains strong. These reunion shows, on Thursday, Dec. 29, and Friday, will provide a rare opportunity to see “The Symphony,” the 1988 Juice Crew posse cut that set a high-water mark for the form, performed live and in full. (Thursday, B. B. King Blues Club & Grill, bbkingblues.com; Friday, Wellmont Theater, Montclair, N.J., wellmonttheater.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE7/25GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1308, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Juice Crew: A Rap History Reunion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Rap History Reunion Shows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Juice Crew (Music Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-21T16:28:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e124ca4c-66b5-5be7-b8c3-4e6b2cdaf922', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e124ca4c-66b5-5be7-b8c3-4e6b2cdaf922'}, {'abstract': 'Noche Flamenca returns with a five-week season at the West-Park Presbyterian Church.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/21/arts/dance/flamenco-with-a-difference-hip-hop-and-antigone-too.html', 'snippet': 'Noche Flamenca returns with a five-week season at the West-Park Presbyterian Church.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The remarkable Noche Flamenca enlists the typical elements of flamenco music and dance — pathos and passion, catharsis and tension — yet part of what makes this company so absorbing is its intimacy. Formed in 1993 by a husband-and-wife team, the director Martín Santangelo and the dancer Soledad Barrio, Noche Flamenca returns with a five-week season at the West-Park Presbyterian Church that features its acclaimed dance-drama “Antigona,” an adaptation of Sophocles’ “Antigone” starring the fiery Ms. Barrio. It’s a part she was born to play.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE4/25GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/25/arts/25GUIDE4/25GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag