y, he should vote to prevent the execution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice Breyer and the Death Penalty', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breyer, Stephen G', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-22T19:41:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/b32b5bd1-29ff-5421-8dbf-b562bdfc7175', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b32b5bd1-29ff-5421-8dbf-b562bdfc7175'}, {'abstract': 'Guillermo del Toro’s new series for Netflix shows his style, but it gets overpowered by that of his partner, DreamWorks Animation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/22/arts/television/review-trollhunters-netflix-guillermo-del-toro.html', 'snippet': 'Guillermo del Toro’s new series for Netflix shows his style, but it gets overpowered by that of his partner, DreamWorks Animation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Guillermo del Toro’s best work — in films like “Pan’s Labyrinth” and “Hellboy 2: The Golden Army” — combines rousing storytelling with a rarely equaled visual splendor. “Trollhunters,” the new series he created for Netflix (based on a book he wrote with Daniel Kraus), signifies two firsts that both seem suited to Mr. del Toro’s protean imagination: his first work in animation and his first project for children.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TROLL/23TROLL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlar