 'Hidden Figures (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Live by Night (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Silence (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-22T22:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By MEG FELLING and ROBIN LINDSAY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'FELLING', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LINDSAY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/cac27812-f3c4-59ff-a538-da99562482ee', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/cac27812-f3c4-59ff-a538-da99562482ee'}, {'abstract': 'The funniest almost-three-hour German comedy you will ever see, Maren Ade’s film is a thrilling act of defiance against the toxicity of doing what is expected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/22/movies/toni-erdmann-review.html', 'snippet': 'The funniest almost-three-hour German comedy you will ever see, Maren Ade’s film is a thrilling act of defiance against the toxicity of doing what is expected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in “Toni Erdmann” — by a wide margin the funniest almost-three-hour German comedy you will ever see — there is a brief discussion, conducted in a sterile modern office building in Bucharest, about the meaning of the English word “performance.” For Anca (Ingrid Bisu), a young Romanian employed by a global consulting firm, it refers to the way she does her job and, more than that, her ability to obey the norms and protocols of the corporate workplace. Her boss, she explains, gives her “a lot of feedback,” which is clearly a euphemism.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23TONI/23TONI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Toni Erdmann,’ Dad’s a Prankster Trying to Jolt His Conformist Daughter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She’s a Conformist. Dad’s a Prankster.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Toni Erdmann (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ade, Maren', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huller, Sandra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simonischek, Peter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-22T22:40:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c69a000-5290-515c-b8a7-3845479cafd7', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c69a000-5290-515c-b8a7-3845479cafd7'}, {'abstract': 'In Martin Scorsese’s film, Jesuit priests in 17th-century Japan operate underground to keep their religion alive when they and their adherents are persecuted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/22/movies/silence-review-martin-scorsese.html', 'snippet': 'In Martin Scorsese’s film, Jesuit priests in 17th-century Japan operate underground to keep their religion alive when they and their adherents are persecuted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Martin Scorsese’s “Silence” is a story of faith and anguish. It tells of a Portuguese Jesuit priest, Father Rodrigues, who in 1643 heads into the dark heart of Japan, where Christians are being persecuted — boiled alive, immolated and crucified. Rodrigues (Andrew Garfield) sets out to help keep the church alive in Japan, a mission that perhaps inevitably leads to God. The film’s solemnity is seductive — as is Mr. Scorsese’s art — especially in light of the triviality and primitiveness of many movies, even if its moments of greatness also make its failures seem more pronounced.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/23/arts/23SILENCE/23SILENCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'lega