section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By N. R. Kleinfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'N.', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Kleinfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01fe4551-05f2-518e-8624-4a89e8759b0d', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01fe4551-05f2-518e-8624-4a89e8759b0d'}, {'abstract': 'If hard drive space is tight on a computer sharing your Dropbox account, you can choose which specific folders you want to see.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/23/technology/personaltech/the-art-of-the-selective-dropbox-sync.html', 'snippet': 'If hard drive space is tight on a computer sharing your Dropbox account, you can choose which specific folders you want to see.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. I keep a ton of stuff in my Dropbox account, which is fine for my desktop computer with its spacious hard disk. I installed the Dropbox app on my ultrabook (instead of just logging in on the web), but it’s nearly filling up my laptop’s available drive space. Is there a way to link to just some of the files — and not everything in the Dropbox folder?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of the Selective Dropbox Sync', 'kicker': 'Tech Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dropbox Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laptop Computers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-23T16:02:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename':