ttps://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/25/arts/design/four-things-to-know-about-donald-judd.html', 'snippet': 'For starters, Judd, who died in 1994, established extensive permanent installations of his work and that of artists he admired in Marfa, Tex.', 'lead_paragraph': '1. The Box. Beginning in the 1960s the artist Donald Judd created a new vocabulary for sculpture by taking it off the plinth and placing it directly on the floor, making the space around the work part of the experience of seeing it. His signature was the box, often deployed serially with subtle variations to emphasize the geometric volumes. Unpainted plywood was a staple of his work, along with Plexiglas and metals like anodized aluminum.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/26JUDDLISTY1/26JUDDLISTY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/26JUDDLISTY1/26JUDDLISTY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/26JUDDLISTY1/26JUDDLISTY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/26JUDDLISTY1/26JUDDLISTY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/26JUDDLISTY1/26JUDDLISTY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/26JUDDLISTY1/26JUDDLISTY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legac