ersons', 'value': 'Dylan, Bob', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hoaxes and Pranks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-26T17:36:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew R. Chow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Chow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96459330-3142-5b7f-924e-c0bdd4aa2ee1', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96459330-3142-5b7f-924e-c0bdd4aa2ee1'}, {'abstract': 'Its deals represented half of all agreements in the worldwide arms bazaar, a study found. The biggest buyers of weapons globally were Qatar, Egypt and Saudi Arabia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/26/us/politics/united-states-global-weapons-sales.html', 'snippet': 'Its deals represented half of all agreements in the worldwide arms bazaar, a study found. The biggest buyers of weapons globally were Qatar, Egypt and Saudi Arabia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The United States again ranked first in global weapons sales last year, signing deals for about $40 billion, or half of all agreements in the worldwide arms bazaar, and far ahead of France, the No. 2 weapons dealer with $15 billion in sales, according to a new congressional study.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Sold $40 Billion in Weapons in 2015, Topping Global Market', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Leads Arms Market With Sales of $40 Billion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shanker, Thom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arms Trade', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Research Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-26T17:42:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36aa780e-9097-5dc2-a65e-623e77d088ef', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36aa780e-9097-5dc2-a65e-623e77d088ef'}, {'abstract': 'When the young singer spoke out about his concerns over “major exposure,” the older legend stepped in with a suggestion: “Loosen up. Swing, man.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/26/arts/music/george-michael-frank-sinatra-letter-fame.html', 'snippet': 'When the young singer spoke out about his concerns over “major exposure,” the older legend stepped in with a suggestion: “Loosen up. Swing, man.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than two decades before he died on Sunday at age 53, George Michael was publicly struggling with the trappings of fame. And when he spoke out about his concerns about the nature of celebrity, Frank Sinatra pulled out his stationery and responded.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/27xp-SINATRA1/27xp-SINATRA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/26/arts/27xp-SINATRA1/27xp-SINATRA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'ran