me': 'Commercial Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Gose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/161413b3-457d-5339-8213-d9b0a7b62b25', 'word_count': 1296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/161413b3-457d-5339-8213-d9b0a7b62b25'}, {'abstract': 'Jacobs & Brichford’s Briana with truffles has an alluring edge, while the Connersville, Ind., farm’s Ameribella has a satin creaminess.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/27/dining/cheese-jacobs-and-brichford-indiana.html', 'snippet': 'Jacobs & Brichford’s Briana with truffles has an alluring edge, while the Connersville, Ind., farm’s Ameribella has a satin creaminess.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the cheeses made by Jacobs & Brichford is a stunner, Briana with truffles. It’s semisoft, almost lacy, and has an alluringly funky edge. The farm in Connersville, Ind., specializes in French cattle breeds whose milk results in cheeses like the prizewinning Everton Premium Reserve, spicy and fairly sharp, with a craggy texture. Other varieties ripen to satin creaminess, like the fruity Ameribella and the mushroomy JQ: Jacobs & Brichford Farmstead Cheeses, $5.75 to $6.75 per quarter pound, jandbcheese.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/28/dining/28BURNER-CHEESE/28BURNER-CHEESE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/28/dining/28BURNER-CHEESE/28BURNER-CHEESE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/28/dining/28BURNER-CHEESE/28BURNER-CHEESE-t