, 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a21124-3969-5a7e-a56b-3d40d0208e35', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a21124-3969-5a7e-a56b-3d40d0208e35'}, {'abstract': 'The American Dance Platform is bringing together eight companies at the Joyce Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/29/arts/dance/how-many-choreographers-does-it-take-to-create-a-duet-16.html', 'snippet': 'The American Dance Platform is bringing together eight companies at the Joyce Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Starting on Tuesday, Jan. 3, the American Dance Platform will gather eight companies from around the country that have a range of influences: jazz, ballet, salsa, modern, Indian and Afro-Cuban dance. The week, curated by Alice B. Adams, of the Kennedy Center in Washington, includes events like an epic duet by 16 choreographers; a dance comparing human relations to a chess game; a tribute to cosmic energy; a satirical depiction of comic book superheroes; and evocations of water — both its grandeur and scarcity. Succeeding programs, each with two attractions, offer companies like Contra-Tiempo (Los Angeles), RAWdance (San Francisco), Ragamala Dance Company (Minneapolis), Davalois Fearon Dance (New York), Dallas Black Dance Theater and Lucky Plush Productions (Chicago). These dancers have brains in their heads as well as steps in their feet. (Through Jan. 8, Joyce Theater; joyce.org/performances.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/arts/01GUIDE9/01GUIDE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/01/01/arts/01GUIDE9/01GUIDE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/arts/01GUIDE9/01GUIDE9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/arts/01GUIDE9/01GUIDE9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 432, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/arts/01GUIDE9/01GUIDE9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1309, 'width': 551, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Many Choreographers Does It Take to Create a Duet? 16', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance; 16 Choreographers, but Just One Duet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joyce Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-29T21:18:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32cc3a5d-abf8-5a6f-8639-e757e84f41a2', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32cc3a5d-abf8-5a6f-8639-e757e84f41a2'}, {'abstract': 'Bartlett Sher and Gianandrea Noseda discuss their production of Gounod’s “Roméo et Juliette,” which opens at the Metropolitan Opera on New Year’s Eve.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/29/arts/music/metropolitan-opera-bartlett-sher-gounod-romeo-et-juliette.html', 'snippet': 'Bartlett Sher and Gianandrea Noseda discuss their production of Gounod’s “Roméo et Juliette,” which opens at the Metropolitan Opera on New Year’s Eve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It might seem like impertinence, not to say blasphemy, to suggest that Shakespeare could ever be bettered. But Bartlett Sher, the director of a new production of Gounod’s “Roméo et Juliette” that opens at the Metropolitan Opera on New Year’s Eve, is willing to go there.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/30gounod-balcony/30gounod-balcony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/30gounod-balcony/30gounod-balcony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/30gounod-balcony/30gounod-balcony-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/30gounod-balcony/30gounod-balcony-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/30gounod-balcony/30gounod-balcony-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/30gounod-balcony/30gounod-balcony-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'ca