ike McPhate', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McPhate', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb59f656-6292-5972-96ca-2116245259bb', 'word_count': 1154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb59f656-6292-5972-96ca-2116245259bb'}, {'abstract': 'The Italian composer and pianist talks about his sold-out concerts, his African influences and playing in the Arctic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/30/arts/music/ludovico-einaudi.html', 'snippet': 'The Italian composer and pianist talks about his sold-out concerts, his African influences and playing in the Arctic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Italian composer and pianist Ludovico Einaudi, 61, is one of the world’s most-streamed classical artists, with more followers on Spotify (about 740,000) than Mozart (680,000). His music, by turns minimal and lush, incorporates an array of influences from Bach to rock to West African kora music. While sold-out audiences across Europe find something sublime in his searching style, some classical critics disagree — like Ben Beaumont-Thomas of The Guardian, who says it’s nothing more than the “dashed-off poignancy of an Instagrammed sunset.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/arts/31einaudi-web/31einaudi-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ludovico Einaudi, a Classical Artist for the YouTube Age', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einaudi, Ludovico', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mozart, Wolfgang Amadeus', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-30T13:21:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a0403a9-f122-5e18-b05a-f761075d69f3', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a0403a9-f122-5e18-b05a-f761075d69f3'}, {'abstract': 'John Grisham, whose “The Whistler” is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, says: “I still pull for the underdog. I still like the little guy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/30/books/review/the-story-behind-this-weeks-best-sellers.html', 'snippet': 'John Grisham, whose “The Whistler” is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, says: “I still pull for the underdog. I still like the little guy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Star Power: This week’s lists reflect book sales for the period ending Dec. 24, which means you’ll find lots of famous novelists on the fiction side of the ledger: In publishing as in home electronics, brand names sell well for the holidays. They know it, too. Here’s John Grisham — whose thriller “The Whistler” remains No. 1 in its ninth week on the hardcover fiction list — describing his target audience in a November interview with Slate: “First of all, she’s female, because two-thirds of all books are bought by women. She’s going to buy it during the Christmas season, because 35 percent of all books are sold during the Christmas season. I would guess she’s probably going to be over 40, because they buy more books than under 40. And that kind of narrows it down. That’s probably my typical reader.” In the same interview, Grisham said he often writes from a sense of injustice about a given subject — don’t be surprised if he tackles student debt in a future thriller — and he gave vent, just a bit, to his political views. The short version: He doesn’t think much of Donald Trump. The slightly longer, more diplomatic version: “I still pull for the underdog. I still like the little guy, especially when the little guy is getting screwed by the big guy: the government, a corporation, an issue, whatever. .\u2008.\u2008. But you’ve got to be careful with your audience, because they don’t always share my politics. You can’t be intrusive with your politics in popular fiction, when you’re trying to entertain people. I really have to watch that, and I do watch it. I don’t feel a responsibility to write a book next year that might change something. My responsibility next year is to write a book that will entertain.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/books/review/01grisham/01grisham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/01/01/books/review/01grisham/01grisham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/books/review/01grisham/01grisham-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/books/review/01grisham/01grisham-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 268, 'width': 261, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/books/review/01grisham/01grisham-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 268, 'width': 261, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Story Behind This Week’s Best Sellers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grisham, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-30T13:30:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba368f92-dd4a-5c08-8e9d-74d3de4703af', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba368f92-dd4a-5c08-8e9d-74d3de4703af'}, {'abstract': 'A Postal Service ad has revived interest in a 1953 Christmas novelty hit by the former child star Gayla Peevey. (She did get the hippopotamus.)', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/30/business/retirement/i-want-a-hippopotamus-for-christmas.html', 'snippet': 'A Postal Service ad has revived interest in a 1953 Christmas novelty hit by the former child star Gayla Peevey. (She did get the hippopotamus.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year’s holiday advertising campaign for the United States Postal Service featured a little girl’s stunningly infectious musical plea: “I Want a Hippopotamus for Christmas.” (And, as she explains, “Only a hippopotamus will do.”) The ditty was, perhaps, unfamiliar to many listeners, but it penetrated the mind space of the unwary at warp speed — and stayed there. Resistance was futile.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Voice of Christmas Past Returns, Asking for a Hippopotamus', 'kicker': 'Retiring', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From a Christmas Long Ago, a Voice Asks for a Hippo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peevey, Gayla (1943- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'I Want a Hippopotamus for Christmas (Song)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Postal Service (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postal Service and Post Offices', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-30T13:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Retirement', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanne Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90587ee9-5543-5fe3-8878-b669358250a5', 'word_count': 1704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90587ee9-5543-5fe3-8878-b669358250a5'}, {'abstract': 'Forecasts about how the market will perform are seldom useful, but there are some bedrock principles that can help investors plan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/30/your-money/after-a-year-of-faulty-predictions-3-approaches-to-investing.html', 'snippet': 'Forecasts about how the market will perform are seldom useful, but there are some bedrock principles that can help investors plan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is one thing analysts can agree on about the year that is coming to a close: 2016 confounded predictions and expectations. From how stocks would perform to who would win the presidency, this was a bad year for people who tell us what tomorrow will bring.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WEALTH3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WEALTH3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WEALTH3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WELATH3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WELATH3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WELATH3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/30/business/31WEALTH3/31WELATH3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1390, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Year of Faulty Predictions, 3 Approaches to Investing', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forget About Forecasts and Go Back to the Fundamentals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Confidence (Economic Indicator)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Stew Leonard's", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2016-12-30T13:36:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/caaf0fd2-317f-5d63-9553-e657ab30f437', 'word_count': 1398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/caaf0fd2-317f-5d63-9553-e657ab30f437'}, {'abstract': 'Having a curb cut doesn’t always guarantee you a parking spot, and it may be more trouble than you bargained for.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/30/realestate/a-new-york-city-driveway-blessing-or-curse.html', 'snippet': 'Having a curb cut doesn’t always guarantee you a parking spot, and it may be more trouble than you bargained for.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many New York City car owners would want nothing more than a designated parking space — such is the desperation many feel in this city so clogged with cars yet so starved for places to put them.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/realestate/01DRIVE/01DRIVE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/01/01/realestate/01DRIVE/01DRIVE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/realestate/01DRIVE/01DRIVE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/realestate/01DRIVE/01DRIVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/01/01/realestate/01DRIVE/01DRIVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/realestate/01DRIVE/01DRIVE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': No