e, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2016-12-31T00:34:36+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/9ef89e39-23e4-5702-bc34-3d7e3056f468', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/9ef89e39-23e4-5702-bc34-3d7e3056f468'}, {'abstract': 'It is easy to become numb to suffering. Feeling empathy for individual victims can overcome the numbness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/30/opinion/to-make-the-world-better-think-small.html', 'snippet': 'It is easy to become numb to suffering. Feeling empathy for individual victims can overcome the numbness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a legend about the comedian W. C. Fields as he lay dying in his hospital bed, surrounded by family. It was winter in New York City, and from outside came the sound of a newsboy shouting, “Wuxtry! Wuxtry! Stock market prices fall!” Stirred out of his stupor, Fields signaled those assembled to his bedside and whispered, “Poor little urchins out there — undernourished, no doubt improperly clad — something’s got to be done about them, something’s got to be done.” He dozed off, but seconds later, opened his eyes again. “On second thought,” he said, “Screw ’em.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/31/opinion/31abrooksWeb/31abrooksWeb-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2016/12/31/opinion/31abrooksWeb/31abrooksWeb-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2016/12/31/opinion/31abrooksWeb/31abrooksW