iginal': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/29e90de8-5499-57e9-99a0-b42105c15b1f', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29e90de8-5499-57e9-99a0-b42105c15b1f'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama has had an impressive run of initiatives that assure him of a lofty perch in the environmental pantheon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/31/opinion/sunday/monuments-man.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama has had an impressive run of initiatives that assure him of a lofty perch in the environmental pantheon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When historians get around to measuring President Obama’s record of protecting America’s public lands from commercial development — its national monuments, parks, forests, wilderness and wildlife refuges — they are likely to rank him high on a list of luminaries that includes both Roosevelts, Jimmy Carter and Bill Clinton. What makes this all the more remarkable is that Mr. Obama came to office as a Chicago urbanite with no obvious passion for environmental stewardship, had no help from Congress and, for his first four years, made little use of his executive authority to protect the federal estate from commercial encroachment. Indeed, his second inaugural address said a great deal about using those lands to produce energy and very little about conservation.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/01/01/opinion/1sun02/1sun02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/01/01/opinion/1sun02/1sun02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype'